{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>On the third day.</b> For there was a distance of three days between them. \"A distance of seven days' journey\" — during all those three days the messenger went to tell Laban that Jacob had gone on his way, so it turns out that he was six days' journey from Laban, and on the seventh Laban overtook him. And we learn that all the distance Jacob traveled in six days, Laban traveled in one day — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 31:23), from Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 74:6). And it is reasonable that Laban would go like a mighty man to run a road, for such is the way of pursuers. But Laban placed a three days' journey between his flocks and the flocks that were in Jacob's hand — not between the city and his flocks, as though Laban pastured his flocks east of the city and Jacob to its west, with a three days' journey between them. Now Jacob departed from the city, for there was his home — his sons and daughters and all that he had, besides the flocks — and it was told to Laban on the third day, for they did not notice on the first day. And Laban returned to his city and took his kinsmen from there, and pursued after him from his city for seven days and overtook him on the seventh day. But let us say according to the view of Bereshit Rabbah that Jacob departed from the field where the flocks were, and Laban took his kinsmen with him from the shearers of his flock. And in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer (chapter 36) it appears that Laban returned to his city, and from there he took every mighty man and every warrior and pursued after him from there."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:41.051560762Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}